  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nSaturday and Saturday night:\n\nThis time period is highly uncertain due to the nature of the\nsetup and model disagreement. \n\nThe overall setup is that shortwave energy and WAA will result\nin potential for thunderstorms across the region (mainly N and W\nof the CWA) with a very unstable airmass in place. With our CWA\non the fringe of better lift and forcing, we will depend more on\noutflow and thunder complex cold pools to bring convection into\nour area, which is very difficult to predict and models handle\npoorly. \n\nSome guidance does suggest a Saturday morning complex diving\ninto the CWA, which could bring a marginal wind threat if this\nmaterializes. The afternoon and evening has the overall best\npotential for severe storms, with the northwestern portions of\nthe CWA being most favored. Threats are still tricky to pin\ndown, but mainly looking at a marginal wind and hail threat,\nwith potential for some stronger gusts and larger hail if things\nline up well.\n\nLate Saturday into early Sunday is also uncertain as the\nenvironment may, depending on earlier convective evolution, \nfavor a storm complex to move southeast into the area. However, \nmodels differ significantly on if and where this could occur. \nWorst case scenario is that a complex of storms moves through, \nbringing primarily a damaging wind threat.\n\nLooking at precip changes through the rest of the long term\nshows almost daily 15-30% chances for showers and storms as we\nwill see SW flow aloft with shortwave energy potential along\nwith broad WAA. \n\nTemperatures will once again be above average. Look for highs in\nthe low to mid 90s Saturday, then the mid to upper 90s Sunday\nthrough Wednesday, with Thursday possibly looking just a little\ncooler.",
